7592186481324 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186481189 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481324 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "db0e353a-fec5-5d61-839e-8dfe38eddf51" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481332 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186480755 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481332 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186480850 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481332 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186481056 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481332 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186481084 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481332 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186481163 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481332 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481332 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481332 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186481163 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481332 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "efe2ad41-143d-5a5f-9acb-57cf8643ab94" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481336 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186480940 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481336 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186480963 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481336 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186481085 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481336 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186481098 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481336 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186481109 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481336 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481336 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481336 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186481085 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481336 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f9685638-c235-52d8-a829-db7d8915b067" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481348 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481245 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481348 #edb/ref 1069 "q-163234" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481348 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481348 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the average value of the function $f(x,y)=6xy$ on the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2{y}^{2},\\,0\\le y\\le 1\\}$ given that the area of the region is $\\frac{2}{3}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481348 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0fd9a9b1-ea5c-5d82-ac2e-6cadd4a02789" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481348 #edb/ref 1074 "Applying the average value formula, we find that the average value of the function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\text{ave}} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{Area}(D)}\\,{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{2}{3})}{∬}_{D}6xy\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2{y}^{2}}6xy\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[3{x}^{2}y]_{x=0}^{x=2{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}12{y}^{5}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}[2{y}^{6}]_{y=0}^{y=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}(2-0) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481357 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481254 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481357 #edb/ref 1069 "q-163257" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481357 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481357 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the average value of the function $f(x,y)={y}^{2}$ on the disk of radius $2$ centered at the origin." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481357 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3b9226c2-67e0-5858-9c64-ae46c4147a95" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481357 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that \n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=\\pi \\cdot {2}^{2}=4\\pi .\n$$\n\nThe average value of the function over a region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{ave}}=\\frac{1}{\\text{Area}(D)}\\,{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nUsing the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,{[\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}]}_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta (4-0)\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{[\\theta -\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta ]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =2[(2\\pi -0)-\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\pi -\\operatorname{sin}⁡0)] \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2\\pi  \\\\  & =4\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{ave}}=\\frac{1}{4\\pi }\\cdot 4\\pi =1.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481367 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481265 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481367 #edb/ref 1069 "q-163197" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481367 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481367 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the function $f(x,y)$ and the region $D$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=2xy,\\,D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le \\frac{y}{2},\\,0\\le y\\le 4\\}.\n$$\n\nAccording to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b)\\in D$ such that the volume of the solid enclosed between the surface $z=f(x,y)$ and the $xy$-plane over the region $D$ equals the volume of the right cylinder with base $D$ and height $f(a,b).$ Find $a$ given that $b=3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481367 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "510822f4-2240-59da-8db4-5275b43b2562" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481367 #edb/ref 1074 "According to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b)\\in D$ such that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A=f(a,b)\\cdot \\text{Area}(D).\n$$\n\nEvaluating the integral on the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{D}2xy\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{y/2}2xy\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}[{x}^{2}y]_{x=0}^{x=y/2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{{y}^{3}}{4}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =[\\frac{{y}^{4}}{16}]_{y=0}^{y=4} \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{y/2}\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}[x]_{x=0}^{x=y/2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{y}{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}]}_{y=0}^{y=4} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we are told that $b=3.$ So, our initial equation simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16 & =f(a,3)\\cdot 4 \\\\ 16 & =(2a\\cdot 3)\\cdot 4 \\\\ 16 & =24a \\\\ a & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $(\\frac{2}{3},3)\\in D.$ Therefore, the answer is $a=\\frac{2}{3}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481378 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481332 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481378 #edb/ref 1069 "q-163208" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481378 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481378 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the function $f(x,y)$ and the region $D$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=3y-2x,\\,D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le y,\\,0\\le y\\le 3\\}.\n$$\n\nAccording to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b)\\in D$ such that the volume of the solid enclosed between the surface $z=f(x,y)$ and the $xy$-plane over the region $D$ equals the volume of the right cylinder with base $D$ and height $f(a,b).$ Find $b$ given that $a=1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481378 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "65bd6053-5f41-5a6f-ac31-815596c779fb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481378 #edb/ref 1074 "According to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b)\\in D$ such that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A=f(a,b)\\cdot \\text{Area}(D).\n$$\n\nEvaluating the integral on the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{D}(3y-2x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{y}(3y-2x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}[3xy-{x}^{2}]_{x=0}^{x=y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}2{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{2{y}^{3}}{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=3} \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{y}\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}[x]_{x=0}^{x=y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=3} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we are told that $a=1.$ So, our initial equation simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}18 & =f(1,b)\\cdot \\frac{9}{2} \\\\ 18 & =(3b-2\\cdot 1)\\cdot \\frac{9}{2} \\\\ 4 & =3b-2 \\\\ 6 & =3b \\\\ b & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $(1,2)\\in D.$ Therefore, the answer is $b=2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481399 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481324 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481399 #edb/ref 1069 "q-163151" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481399 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481399 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the function $f(x,y)$ and the region $D$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=2x+{y}^{2},\\,D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 3\\}.\n$$\n\nAccording to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b)\\in D$ such that the volume of the solid enclosed between the surface $z=f(x,y)$ and the $xy$-plane over the region $D$ equals the volume of the right cylinder with base $D$ and height $f(a,b).$ Find $a$ given that $b=1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481399 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a130ee36-c4fa-557a-b62c-af4b4bf44ed4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481399 #edb/ref 1074 "According to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b)\\in D$ such that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A=f(a,b)\\cdot \\text{Area}(D).\n$$\n\nEvaluating the integral on the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{D}(2x+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}(2x+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[2xy+\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}]_{y=0}^{y=3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(6x+9)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[3{x}^{2}+9x]_{x=0}^{x=2} \\\\  & =(3\\cdot {2}^{2}+9\\cdot 2)-0 \\\\  & =30.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=2\\cdot 3=6.\n$$\n\nAlso, we are told that $b=1.$ So, our initial equation simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}30 & =f(a,1)\\cdot 6 \\\\ 30 & =(2a+{1}^{2})\\cdot 6 \\\\ 30 & =12a+6 \\\\ 24 & =12a \\\\ a & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $(2,1)\\in D.$ Therefore, the answer is $a=2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481402 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481336 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481402 #edb/ref 1069 "q-163231" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481402 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481402 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the average value of the function $f(x,y)=3{x}^{2}-y$ on the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,1\\le y\\le 3\\}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481402 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a3d12aa9-7854-5ac5-a883-81ef95d3c722" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481402 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that \n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=2\\cdot 2=4.\n$$\n\nApplying the average value formula, we find that the average value of the function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\text{ave}} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{Area}(D)}\\,{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{∬}_{D}(3{x}^{2}-y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}(3{x}^{2}-y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{[3{x}^{2}y-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=1}^{y=3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(6{x}^{2}-4)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[2{x}^{3}-4x]_{x=0}^{x=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(2\\cdot {2}^{3}-4\\cdot 2\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{4} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481403 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481246 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481403 #edb/ref 1069 "q-163149" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481403 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481403 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the function $f(x,y)$ and the region $D$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=2x+3{y}^{2},\\,D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2\\}.\n$$\n\nAccording to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b)\\in D$ such that the volume of the solid enclosed between the surface $z=f(x,y)$ and the $xy$-plane over the region $D$ equals the volume of the right cylinder with base $D$ and height $f(a,b).$ Find $a$ given that $b=1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481403 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a8b13a1a-6bdb-56d5-b06e-dfa9901c1db1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481403 #edb/ref 1074 "According to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b)\\in D$ such that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A=f(a,b)\\cdot \\text{Area}(D).\n$$\n\nEvaluating the integral on the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{D}(2x+3{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(2x+3{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[2xy+{y}^{3}]_{y=0}^{y=2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(4x+8)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[2{x}^{2}+8x]_{x=0}^{x=2} \\\\  & =(2\\cdot {2}^{2}+8\\cdot 2)-0 \\\\  & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=2\\cdot 2=4.\n$$\n\nAlso, we are told that $b=1.$ So, our initial equation simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}24 & =f(a,1)\\cdot 4 \\\\ 24 & =(2a+3\\cdot {1}^{2})\\cdot 4 \\\\ 6 & =2a+3 \\\\ 3 & =2a \\\\ a & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $(\\frac{3}{2},1)\\in D.$ Therefore, the answer is $a=\\frac{3}{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481407 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481282 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481407 #edb/ref 1069 "q-163277" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481407 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481407 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/06/06a853735916ae3219eea02fa7e7e30d9e941106600cd5b642d951f553aace59.png)\n\nFind the average value of the function $f(x,y)=5y({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})$ on the semicircle shown above." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481407 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b0a81f04-6f59-512d-aa20-c54e3b26a51d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481407 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that \n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\pi \\cdot {1}^{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nThe average value of the function over a region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{ave}}=\\frac{1}{\\text{Area}(D)}\\,{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $1$ centered at \n$(1,0).$\n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that is mind, let's now write the $y=-\\sqrt{2x-{x}^{2}}$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\sqrt{2x-{x}^{2}} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =2x-{x}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =2x \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} & =2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) & =2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2} & =2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2}-2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\nTherefore, in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le 0\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}5y({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}5r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ((r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2})\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }5{r}^{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }5{r}^{4}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta [{r}^{5}]_{0}^{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ((2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{5}-0)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =32{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{5}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =32{[-\\frac{1}{6}{cos}^{6}⁡\\theta ]}_{-\\pi /2}^{0} \\\\  & =-\\frac{16}{3}[{cos}^{6}⁡(0)-{cos}^{6}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})] \\\\  & =-\\frac{16}{3}(1-0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{16}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{ave}}=\\frac{1}{(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}\\cdot (-\\frac{16}{3})=-\\frac{32}{3\\pi }.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481408 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481317 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481408 #edb/ref 1069 "q-163256" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481408 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481408 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the average value of the function $f(x,y)={e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ on the disk of radius $\\frac{1}{2}$ centered at the origin." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481408 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b2ff4fa5-a334-50b5-9869-f8e569ec5011" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481408 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that \n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=\\pi \\cdot {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nThe average value of the function over a region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{ave}}=\\frac{1}{\\text{Area}(D)}\\,{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le \\frac{1}{2},\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nUsing the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}{e}^{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{e}^{{r}^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{[\\frac{{e}^{{r}^{2}}}{2}]}_{0}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(\\frac{{e}^{1/4}}{2}-\\frac{1}{2})\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt[4]{e}-1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt[4]{e}-1}{2}[\\theta ]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt[4]{e}-1}{2}(2\\pi -0) \\\\  & =(\\sqrt[4]{e}-1)\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{ave}}=\\frac{4}{\\pi }\\cdot (\\sqrt[4]{e}-1)\\pi =4(\\sqrt[4]{e}-1).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481411 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481322 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481411 #edb/ref 1069 "q-163240" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481411 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481411 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the average value of the function $f(x,y)=y+{x}^{2}$ on the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,x\\le y\\le 2x\\}$ given that the area of the region is $2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481411 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bbfd9077-09aa-55c5-a7ec-ce238f7922af" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481411 #edb/ref 1074 "Applying the average value formula, we find that the average value of the function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\text{ave}} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{Area}(D)}\\,{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{∬}_{D}y+{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{x}^{2x}y+{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}+{x}^{2}y]_{y=x}^{y=2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}[{x}^{3}+\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2}]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2}]_{x=0}^{x=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(8-0) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481421 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481276 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481421 #edb/ref 1069 "q-163205" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481421 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481421 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the function $f(x,y)$ and the region $D$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=2x+y,\\,D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,\\frac{y}{2}\\le x\\le y,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 6\\}.\n$$\n\nAccording to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b)\\in D$ such that the volume of the solid enclosed between the surface $z=f(x,y)$ and the $xy$-plane over the region $D$ equals the volume of the right cylinder with base $D$ and height $f(a,b).$ Find $a$ given that $b=4.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481421 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d1845eab-f52b-5f23-bd27-faf40f79c71a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481421 #edb/ref 1074 "According to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b)\\in D$ such that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A=f(a,b)\\cdot \\text{Area}(D).\n$$\n\nEvaluating the integral on the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{D}(2x+y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{6}{\\int }_{y/2}^{y}(2x+y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{6}[{x}^{2}+xy]_{x=y/2}^{x=y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{6}([{y}^{2}+{y}^{2}]-[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}])\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{6}\\frac{5{y}^{2}}{4}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{5{y}^{3}}{12}]}_{y=0}^{y=6} \\\\  & =90.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{6}{\\int }_{y/2}^{y}\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{6}[x]_{x=y/2}^{x=y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{6}\\frac{y}{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}]}_{y=0}^{y=6} \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we are told that $b=4.$ So, our initial equation simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}90 & =f(a,4)\\cdot 9 \\\\ 90 & =(2a+4)\\cdot 9 \\\\ 10 & =2a+4 \\\\ 6 & =2a \\\\ a & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $(3,4)\\in D.$ Therefore, the answer is $a=3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
